<!--
  Every package the Windows app takes from NuGet, and at which version: all of them,
  in this one file. A project names the package it wants and nothing more; the version
  comes from here. So this is the whole list of what the app depends on that is not
  Microsoft's own .NET, and a line added here is a new dependency, which wants the
  maintainer's yes first (AGENTS.md).

  They are the ones spec/10-platforms.md names:

    Google.Protobuf   Reads and writes a .pro file. It is in the app.
    Grpc.Tools        Makes the C# that does so from proto/autogen-proto, at each
                      build (ProFormat/ProFormat.csproj). It has protoc in it, so none
                      has to be installed. It is used to build and is not in the app.
    xunit.v3          The tests, and what runs them: each project of tests is a
                      program that runs itself, which `dotnet test` starts (global.json
                      says how). It is in the tests and not in the app.

  Google.Protobuf must not be older than the protoc inside Grpc.Tools: code made by a
  newer protoc may ask the library for what an older one has not got. (Grpc.Tools
  2.84.0 has protoc 35.1, which is Google.Protobuf 3.35.1's.)

  Not here, and not needed by `dotnet test`: xunit.runner.visualstudio and
  Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, which are for the older way of running tests. If Visual
  Studio's own Test Explorer ever fails to list these tests, those two are the first
  thing to look at, and adding them is a new dependency to ask about.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Google.Protobuf" Version="3.36.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.Tools" Version="2.84.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
